<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">U. N. Dhebar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Uchharangrai Navalshankar Dhebar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">U. N. Dhebar was an Indian politician from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a> who served as the first chief minister of Saurashtra State from 1948 to 1954. A follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> active in the movement for popular government in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, he led the administration from Rajkot during the merger of the Kathiawar states. He was president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> from 1955 to 1959.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/u-n-dhebar.jpg" alt="U. N. Dhebar" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U._N._Dhebar.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dhebar was born on 21 September 1905 in the hamlet of Gangajala, eleven miles from Jamnagar, and practised as a lawyer after his university education. Under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, and in opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1935">Government of India Act 1935</a>, he gave up the law in 1936 to join the independence movement in Rajkot. He became president of the Viramgam Taluka Congress Committee and of the Rajkot Mill Kamdar Mandal, secretary of the Kathiawar Political Conference in 1937 and 1938, and president of the Rajkot Praja Mandal in 1938 and 1939.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He led the Rajkot Satyagraha against the ruler of Rajkot State, whose heavy taxes and limits on speech and assembly had angered his subjects, and was imprisoned from 1938 to 1939. In January 1941 he joined the Individual Satyagraha against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> and spent six months in the Sabarmati jail. He then worked in the Confederation Movement, which sought to group the small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> under larger ones. Arrested on 9 August 1942 on his way back from the All India Congress Committee meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, he was held in Rajkot until 31 August 1945.</p><p>When the Nawab of Junagadh agreed in October 1947 to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, Dhebar declared a boycott of all goods going to and from the state, and after about a month and a half its ruler went into hiding. Saurashtra State was formed on 15 February 1948 from what had been 202 princely and administrative units, and Dhebar became its chief minister that day. His government's early years went on political, administrative and financial integration, including the transfer of customs revenue to the central government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In November 1954 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> brought him onto the national stage, and he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a> to serve as president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> from January 1955 to February 1959. He sought to bring farmers, craftsmen, labourers and students into the party, headed its planning committee, and urged the South African Indian Congress to keep to non-violence in its struggle against apartheid. Afterwards he chaired the Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes Commission in 1960 and 1961, entered the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lok-sabha">Lok Sabha</a> from Rajkot in 1962, and chaired the Khadi and Village Industries Commission. He received the Padma Vibhushan in 1973 and died on 11 March 1977.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “U. N. Dhebar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jam Sahib of Nawanagar, Digvijaysinhji, served as Rajpramukh, while an elected government led by <b>U. N. Dhebar</b> ran the administration from Rajkot.</blockquote><a href="/state/saurashtra-state">Saurashtra State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jamnagar district, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Rajkot, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.25,70&amp;year=1951" aria-label="Jamnagar district on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6425 1342 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of U. N. Dhebar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6875 1612h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6875 1612h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.25,70&amp;year=1951">What was here in 1951? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
